{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on the exact-head workflow guard at exact head b548c0e9bd8020bfa8a31aeb84f44a172f093f5e, against request 99e08a66b50b8409711d148b88d01fbb5bf9947a and promise dfcf0d13627b5900457cbf3eaee2270b7f07db2a. Worktree clean at that head; make vet, make test (12 packages), make race, make build and git diff --check all pass.\n\nEvery guard you name is real. I exercised the built binaries in disposable workrooms end to end — init, actor-add, request, promise, artifact, review, ratify, merge — and each refusal fires at its named boundary with a positive control proving the harness can succeed. Dirty checkout, advanced head, wrong-actor promise, foreign repository, bad verdict word, promise-passed-as-artifact, unratified approval, abbreviated candidate, candidate-not-equal-to-approved-head, dirty target, non-report approval: all refused, and the clean paths sign and merge. The documentation is careful and I found no overclaim in it: it enumerates what the commands refuse, and they refuse exactly that.\n\nThe finding is coverage. Five of the nine guards I could isolate are green under build-valid mutation, and two of them are the ones that carry the meaning of the whole tool.\n\nThe first is the verdict check in validateMerge. Delete `approval.Body[\"verdict\"] != \"approved\"` and the entire suite passes. That is the single line separating *reviewed* from *approved* — with it gone, a ratified CHANGES-REQUESTED report merges. The behaviour is correct today: I built the case live, signed a changes-requested verdict, had the requester ratify it, and merge refused with \"review verdict is not approved\" and nothing landed. But the string \"changes-requested\" does not appear once in cmd/gs/main_test.go, so nothing holds that line in place. For a tool whose entire purpose is refusing to merge unapproved work, that is the guard I would least like to lose to a refactor.\n\nThe second is promise ownership in validateReview. Neutering `promise.Actor != actor.Fingerprint` is also green. Live, it refuses correctly — alice cannot sign on bob's promise — but nothing pins it, and it is what stops one participant signing a verdict on another's commitment.\n\nThe other three green: the same-repository checkout check, the approval-rests-on-its-named-artifact check, and the re-validation comparison before signing. That last one is worth a note of its own: it exists to close the check-to-sign window, and no test can currently distinguish a build where it works from one where it does not.\n\nOn the windows you asked me to assess. Review is sound in the way that matters, and not because of the double read: the signed report records the artifact's immutable commit, so a checkout that moves after validation cannot retarget the verdict. What the guard actually proves is that the checkout was at that commit when the verdict was signed — not that the reviewer read that commit — and the docs say precisely this (\"guards the state at which the verdict is signed\"), which is the right claim. Merge has a genuine residual window: between the second validateMerge and `git merge` the approval could be superseded, and nothing rechecks. It cannot be closed without coupling the durable read to the Git write, and I would rather it be stated than closed badly.\n\nTwo properties that are not windows but standing facts, and I think they should be decided rather than left implicit. A ratified approval is never consumed: I merged the same approved commit under the same approval into three different branches, all exit 0. And the merge target is unconstrained — validateCheckout is called with requireHead false for merge, correctly, since HEAD is the target, but the consequence is that an approval authorising a commit authorises it into anything. Combined with `gs merge` emitting no durable act, the log can say a commit was approved but never where it landed. The docs do tell the operator to record the merge artifact separately, so this is disclosed by omission rather than misstated — but \"was main's content approved\" is not a question the record can answer, and that seems close to the point of the exercise.\n\nTwo disclosures about my own method, because both would have produced false findings. My first end-to-end harness built an invalid artifact — I had omitted the required body.path and body.to fields — so every refusal came back \"artifact is not effective\" and looked like six guards firing. The positive controls failed, which is the only reason I caught it; without them I would have reported a working guard suite from a harness that could never have signed anything. Then my first mutation batch reported five guards \"caught\" that were actually Go build failures — `declared and not used` after neutering a condition. I re-ran every mutation behind an explicit `go build` gate and reworked each to keep its variables live; four of those five turned out to be green. Both errors have the same shape as the ones I have been reporting to you, which is not lost on me.\n\nTo close: test the verdict check with a ratified changes-requested report, and the promise-ownership check with a foreign promise — both are cheap and both are already reachable by the harness your existing tests use. Then either pin the same-repository, rests-on-artifact and re-validation guards or say plainly that they are defence in depth. And I would like a decision recorded on approval replay and merge-target scope, either as intended behaviour or as work, rather than leaving it to be discovered by whoever merges an approved commit into the wrong branch.","body":{"branch":"task/exact-head-workflow-guard","head":"b548c0e9bd8020bfa8a31aeb84f44a172f093f5e","verdict":"CHANGES-REQUESTED"}}